uler","value_qid":"Q40110","value_label":"Muhammad Jamalul Alam II","from_t":1906.3534246575343,"to_t":1924.6939890710382,"title":"Sultan of Brunei Darussalam","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Muhammad Jamalul Alam II"},{"qid":"Q921","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q40111","value_label":"Ahmad Tajuddin of Brunei","from_t":1924.6939890710382,"to_t":1950.4191780821918,"title":"Sultan of Brunei Darussalam","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Ahmad Tajuddin of Brunei"},{"qid":"Q921","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q40390","value_label":"Abdul Momin of Brunei","from_t":1852,"to_t":1885.4082191780822,"title":"Sultan of Brunei Darussalam","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Abdul Momin of Brunei"},{"qid":"Q921","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q40512","value_label":"Omar Ali Saifuddien III of Brunei","from_t":1950.4219178082192,"to_t":1967.7561643835616,"title":"Sultan of Brunei Darussalam","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Omar Ali Saifuddien III of Brunei"},{"qid":"Q921","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q57327","value_label":"Hassanal Bolkiah I of Brunei","from_t":1967.7589041095891,"to_t":null,"title":"Sultan of Brunei Darussalam","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Hassanal Bolkiah I of Brunei"},{"qid":"Q921","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q7443078","value_label":"Muhammad Kanzul Alam of Brunei","from_t":1807,"to_t":1826,"title":"Sultan of Brunei Darussalam","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Muhammad Kanzul Alam of Brunei"},{"qid":"Q921","kind":"flag","value_qid":"Q165872","value_label":"Flag of Brunei.svg","from_t":1959.7424657534248,"to_t":null,"title":"flag of Brunei","property":"P163","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"flag of Brunei"}],"people":{"Q57327":{"label":"Hassanal Bolkiah I of Brunei","description":"Sultan of Brunei since 1967","enwiki":"Hassanal Bolkiah","summary":"Hassanal Bolkiah Muiz'zaddin Wad'daulah (born 15 July 1946) has reigned as the sultan of Brunei since 1967, and has also served as the prime minister of Brunei since its independence from British rule in 1984. He is among the world's few remaining absolute monarchs.\nThe eldest son of Sultan Omar Ali Saifuddien III and Raja Isteri Pengiran Anak Damit, he is the 29th sultan to ascend to the Bruneian throne, following the abdication of his father in 1967. The Sultan has been ranked among the wealthiest individuals in the world.","image":"Visita de Estado del Sultán de Brunéi Darussalam, Hassanal Bolkiah (cropped).jpg","birth":1946.5342465753424,"death":null},"Q12485977":{"label":"Husin Kamaluddin of Brunei","description":"Sultan of Brunei","enwiki":"Husin Kamaluddin","summary":"Husin Kamaluddin ibnu Muhammad Ali (died c. 1770) or also known as Sultan Kamaluddin Malik Al Dzahir, was the 16th Sultan of Brunei. Following his death, he was posthumously given the title of Marhum Di Luba as he lives at Luba. He is descended from the 12th Sultan of Brunei, Sultan Muhammad Ali, as the second son. The nation flourished during his rule, food was easily obtained, and he also introduced Brunei pitis, the nation's first unit of money.","image":null,"birth":1650,"death":1740},"Q12709880":{"label":"Muhammad Alam of Brunei","description":"Sultan of Brunei","enwiki":"Muhammad Alam","summary":"Muhammad Alam ibnu Muhammad Kanzul Alam (died c. 1828) was the self-proclaimed 22nd sultan of Brunei from 1826 to 1828, the year he is alleged to have died. Due to his tough and strict attitude, he was given the Malay nicknames Raja Api (King of Fire) and Sultan Marak Berapi. Despite being perceived as a fragile and progressively protective kingdom, Brunei managed to hold onto its lands under his rule, which stretched from Tanjong Datu in Sarawak to Kimanis in Sabah.\nHis rise to power as the Sultan was disliked by most of the population of Brunei and ignored royal Bruneian tradition which led to the second civil war between him and his successor, Pengiran Muda Omar Ali Saifuddin.","image":null,"birth":1784,"death":1828},"Q20448054":{"label":"Nasruddin of Brunei","description":"Sultan of Brunei","enwiki":"Nasruddin of Brunei","summary":"Nasruddin ibni Pengiran Muda Besar Abdullah (died 1710) was the sultan of Brunei from 1690 to 1710, and the grandson of Abdul Jalilul Akbar. Nasruddin is described as a smart monarch who was very interested in state government and administration.","image":null,"birth":null,"death":1710},"Q40101":{"label":"Hashim Jalilul Alam Aqamaddin of Brunei","description":"Sultan of Brunei (1825-1906)","enwiki":"Hashim Jalilul Alam Aqamaddin","summary":"Hashim Jalilul Alam Aqamaddin (1825 – 10 May 1906) was the 25th Sultan of Brunei from 1885 until his death in 1906. Pengiran Anak Hashim or Hashim Jalilul was a prominent and controversial figure in Bruneian history. Many Western visitors' narrative painted him and his surroundings in a bad way, which was indeed consistent with the idea that Brunei was a decaying monarchy at the time.\nSultan Hashim requested British help in the late 19th century to stop Rajah Charles Brooke's territorial expansions, which resulted in the 1888 Protectorate Agreement.","image":"Hashim Jalilul Alam Aqamaddin.jpg","birth":1825,"death":1906.3534246575343},"Q40107":{"label":"Omar Ali Saifuddin II of Brunei","description":"Sultan of Brunei (1799-1852)","enwiki":"Omar Ali Saifuddin II","summary":"Omar Ali Saifuddin II (died 18 November 1852) was the 23rd Sultan of Brunei from 1828 until his death in 1852. During his reign, Western powers such as the United Kingdom and the United States visited the country. His reign saw the British adventurer James Brooke becoming the White Rajah of Sarawak.","image":"Illustration of Omar Ali Saifuddin II.jpg","birth":1799.0904109589042,"death":1852.8852459016393},"Q40110":{"label":"Muhammad Jamalul Alam II","description":"Sultan of Brunei (1889-1924)","enwiki":"Muhammad Jamalul Alam II","summary":"Muhammad Jamalul Alam II (1889 – 19 September 1924) was the 26th Sultan of Brunei from 1906 until his death in 1924. He was succeeded by his eldest son Ahmad Tajuddin.\nJamalul Alam was the first Bruneian sultan to speak English, reflecting British influence as Brunei navigated its relationship with the British Residency established in 1906. Initially, the Sultan encountered resistance from conservative royal family members opposed to British administrative reforms.","image":"Sultan Muhammad Jamalul Alam II(Brunei).jpg","birth":1889,"death":1924.6939890710382},"Q40111":{"label":"Ahmad Tajuddin of Brunei","description":"Sultan of Brunei (1913-1950)","enwiki":"Ahmad Tajuddin","summary":"Ahmad Tajuddin Akhazul Khairi Waddien (22 August 1912 – 4 June 1950) was the 27th Sultan of Brunei from 1924 until his death in 1950. He was succeeded by his younger brother Sultan Omar Ali Saifuddien III.\nAhmad Tajuddin has been disregarded by Malcolm MacDonald and frequently ignored and mishandled by others like his political advisor Gerard MacBryan. He advocated for the Sultanate to have more financial and political autonomy in a way that was innovative for his day. This, along with his support for a new political confederation governed by the Sultanate over northern Borneo, foreshadowed much of the political process that started in the late 1950s and culminated in January 1984 with the official declaration of Brunei's independence from Britain.","image":"Sultan Ahmad Tajuddin Akhazul Khairi Waddien.jpg","birth":1913.6383561643836,"death":1950.4191780821918},"Q40390":{"label":"Abdul Momin of Brunei","description":"Sultan of Brunei (1788-1885)","enwiki":"Abdul Momin","summary":"Abdul Momin ibni Abdul Wahab (died 1885) was the 24th Sultan of Brunei from 1852 until his death in 1885. Abdul Momin sought British support as he blocked Charles Brooke's attempts to acquire more Bruneian land in 1868. In 1877, Baron von Overbeck secured Sabah’s concession of land from Abdul Momin for an annual payment. Brooke's manipulative tactics ultimately led to British restrictions on his land acquisitions while Overbeck gained control over northern Borneo.","image":"Illustration of Sultan Abdul Mumin.jpg","birth":1788.3852459016393,"death":1885.4054794520548},"Q40512":{"label":"Omar Ali Saifuddien III of Brunei","description":"Sultan of Brunei (1914-1986)","enwiki":"Omar Ali Saifuddien III","summary":"Omar Ali Saifuddien III Sa'adul Khairi Waddien (Jawi: عمر علي سيف الدين سعد الخير والدين) ((1914-09-23)23 September 1914 to 7 September 1986(1986-09-07) (aged 71)), born into the House of Bolkiah, reigned as the Sultan of Brunei Darassalam from 1950 until his abdication in 1967.","image":"Omar Ali Saifuddien III.jpg","birth":1914.7260273972602,"death":1986.682191780822},"Q7443078":{"label":"Muhammad Kanzul Alam of Brunei","description":"Sultan of Brunei","enwiki":"Muhammad Kanzul Alam","summary":"Muhammad Kanzul Alam ibni Omar Ali Saifuddin I (died 1826) was the sultan of Brunei from 1807 until his death in 1826.\nKanzul Alam succeeded Muhammad Tajuddin as sultan of Brunei in 1807, but since he had broken a contract with his predecessor, his rule was marked by internal conflict. Even though Omar Ali Saifuddin II still possessed the royal regalia, his son Muhammad Alam felt entitled to the throne as he rose to prominence during his own rule. Perhaps as a result of Muhammad Alam's severe and autocratic actions, a civil war broke out between him and his followers after Kanzul Alam's death in 1826.","image":null,"birth":1750,"death":1829}},"media":{"Old Flag of Brunei.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Old_Flag_of_Brunei.svg/330px-Old_Flag_of_Brunei.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Flag_of_Brunei.svg","author":"The original uploader was  at .\n\n(Original text : Orange Tuesday (talk))","license":"Public domain"},"Flag of Brunei.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Flag_of_Brunei.svg/330px-Flag_of_Brunei.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Brunei.svg","author":"Nightstallion","license":"Public domain"},"Emblem of Brunei.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Emblem_of_Brunei.svg/330px-Emblem_of_Brunei.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblem_of_Brunei.svg","author":"extracted from the flag of Brunei, provided at the Open Clip Art website.","license":"CC0"},"Visita de Estado del Sultán de Brunéi Darussalam, Hassanal Bolkiah (cropped).jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Visita_de_Estado_del_Sult%C3%A1n_de_Brun%C3%A9i_Darussalam%2C_Hassanal_Bolkiah_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Visita_de_Estado_del_Sult%C3%A1n_de_Brun%C3%A9i_Darussalam%2C_Hassanal_Bolkiah_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Visita_de_Estado_del_Sult%C3%A1n_de_Brun%C3%A9i_Darussalam,_Hassanal_Bolkiah_(cropped).jpg","author":"Cancillería del Perú","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0"},"Hashim Jalilul Alam Aqamaddin.jpg":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d2/Hashim_Jalilul_Alam_Aqamaddin.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hashim_Jalilul_Alam_Aqamaddin.jpg","author":"Bender21435","license":"Public domain"},"Illustration of Omar Ali Saifuddin II.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Illustration_of_Omar_Ali_Saifuddin_II.jpg/330px-Illustration_of_Omar_Ali_Saifuddin_II.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Illustration_of_Omar_Ali_Saifuddin_II.jpg","author":"Pusat Sejarah Brunei","license":"Public domain"},"Sultan Muhammad Jamalul Alam II(Brunei).jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Sultan_Muhammad_Jamalul_Alam_II%28Brunei%29.jpg/330px-Sultan_Muhammad_Jamalul_Alam_II%28Brunei%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sultan_Muhammad_Jamalul_Alam_II(Brunei).jpg","author":"Brunei History Center","license":"Public domain"},"Sultan Ahmad Tajuddin Akhazul Khairi Waddien.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Sultan_Ahmad_Tajuddin_Akhazul_Khairi_Waddien.jpg/330px-Sultan_Ahmad_Tajuddin_Akhazul_Khairi_Waddien.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sultan_Ahmad_Tajuddin_Akhazul_Khairi_Waddien.jpg","author":"Unknown authorUnknown author","license":"Public domain"},"Illustration of Sultan Abdul Mumin.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Illustration_of_Sultan_Abdul_Mumin.jpg/330px-Illustration_of_Sultan_Abdul_Mumin.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Illustration_of_Sultan_Abdul_Mumin.jpg","author":"Unknown authorUnknown author","license":"Public domain"},"Omar Ali Saifuddien III.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Omar_Ali_Saifuddien_III.jpg/330px-Omar_Ali_Saifuddien_III.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Omar_Ali_Saifuddien_III.jpg","author":"Unknown authorUnknown author","license":"Public domain"}},"names":["Brunei","Brunei İmparatorluğu","Bruneian Empire","Bruneian Sultanate","Bruneiisches Großreich","Bruneium","Bruney","Brunéi","Brunéi Darussalam","Empire de Brunei","Empire of Brunei","Estado de Bruney Darussalam","Estado de Brunéi, Morada de la Paz","Historia Brunei","Imperio de Brun